comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-05T18:18:10.921Z","harness:claude-code","Reproduced 2026-08-05 on this checkout:\n\n  pm contracts --json        -> commands: [] (length 0), command_summaries: 76 records,\n                                omission_receipt.has_omissions = true,\n                                omitted_field_groups = [{name: full_contract_catalog, restore_with: --full}]\n  pm contracts --full --json -> commands: 160 bare strings, command_summaries: 0,\n                                omission_receipt.has_omissions = false\n\nThe declared row_contract does keep an agent honest if it uses the published jq_selector: row_keys is [command_summaries, commands] by default and [commands] under --full, so the selector returns the right rows in both. The defect is not that rows are unreachable — it is that the receipt asserts completeness on the projection that discarded the structured records, and that one key name carries two incompatible row shapes.\n\nThe practical cost for an agent: the default projection is where per-command intent and default_max_estimated_tokens live, and those are exactly the fields an agent needs to budget a call. Reaching for --full to get more loses them, and nothing in the response says so."
  "2026-08-06T06:53:58.797Z","harness:codex","Implementation evidence: contracts --full now includes the same command_summaries and output_policy as summary mode while retaining schema, flags, runtime, MCP, context, read, error, and output contracts. Focused monotonicity regression, manual fresh-workspace proof (55 active no-extension summaries plus full surfaces), static/token/context gates, and exact full coverage pass."
